tops": 0, "is_error": false, "num_turns": 6, "subtype": "success", "api_error_status": null, "result": "The spec is implemented and its required check passes: `pytest -q tests/test_create_table_parser.py tests/test_introspect.py tests/test_transform.py` gives 164 passed. The full test suite also passes (1416 passed, 16 skipped). I reviewed the changes to `db.py` closely and only looked at the outline of the new parser. No fixes were needed. mypy and ruff aren't installed in the venv, so type checks and linting weren't run.\n\n**What changed:**\n- **New `sqlite_utils/create_table_parser.py`** (standard library only): reads a `CREATE TABLE` statement to find its CHECK constraints and the comments attached to each column. It also has the helpers for spotting and renaming columns inside a CHECK expression.\n- **`sqlite_utils/db.py`:**\n  - Tables now have `checks`, `column_checks` and `table_checks` properties.\n  - `.transform()` now keeps CHECK constraints and column comments when it rebuilds a table. It renames columns inside CHECK expressions, and drops a constraint or comment along with the column it belongs to.\n  - `.transform()` refuses to drop a column that a remaining constraint still uses, and stops with an error before changing anything.\n\n**Choices the implementer made where the spec was silent:**\n- `parse_column_comments` returns `{}` for virtual tables and for SQL that isn't a `CREATE TABLE`. A malformed `CREATE TABLE` still raises `ParseError`.\n- Renaming matches the keys of the rename mapping without regard to case.\n- If a column is written as `[name]` and the new name contains `]`, it switches to double quotes, because SQLite has no way to escape `]` inside brackets.\n\nNothing is committed; the changes are in the working tree.", "ttft_ms": 2540, "type": "result", "duration_ms": 379847, "uuid": "a910e81f-5581-4ef3-9859-ce51c25cc035", "ttft_stream_ms": 1950, "time_to_request_ms": 402, "first_content_frame_ms": 1950, "queued_turn_count": 0, "result_index": 0}
